  "enhanced_information": "The clinical presentation of a 38-year-old woman with fatigue, pruritus, and an enlarged, nontender liver, along with elevated alkaline phosphatase and positive antimitochondrial antibodies, is highly suggestive of primary biliary cholangitis (PBC). In PBC, liver biopsy typically reveals lymphocytic infiltration of portal areas and periductal granulomas, which aligns with the consensus on the histopathological findings associated with this autoimmune liver disease. Other options, such as fibrous obliteration of bile ducts or hepatocyte degeneration, do not correspond with the expected findings in PBC. Therefore, the most likely biopsy finding in this case is lymphocytic infiltration of portal areas and periductal granulomas."
